 The Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) is the third enabler (Chapter~\ref{ch:database}). It organizes the sensory-afferent literature into a structured afferent resource \citep{bolen_sensory_2023}: of the 561 papers retained after the audit and the prune, 523 carry structured curation notes, 86 carry an afferent classification, and 258 carry notes translating each paper toward robot or simulation use, and the full audited corpus of 943 papers, including the 382 pruned records preserved under an archived flag, remains reconstructible in the packaged tool. The toolset matters as much as the corpus. Its detailed table drills from any filter down to a paper's full notes; its pivot view counts the corpus along any two dimensions at once; its bubble map draws every paper as a neuron, soma sized by citations, with citation links rendered as excitatory and inhibitory synapses in the laboratory's convention and a focus mode that walks the citation neighborhood. The monthly literature-scraping extension is under development; it is separate from the completed curation and application described here. The evaluation of AI-assisted literature triage established the division of labor that makes expansion affordable \citep{bolen_using_2024}: the machine drafts, and the human rules.
 
-The fourth enabler is the set of novel neuromechanical simulation tools (Chapter~\ref{ch:sns}): the audited OpenSim-to-MuJoCo conversion of the robot's musculoskeletal model, the force interface that computes actuator forces inside the loop from the validated BPA model, the two-level spinal networks built in SNS-Toolbox, the Simulink SNS library for assembly in a controls-engineering environment, the cross-engine verification harness, and the spiking-neuron mirrors that test the coding assumption. On these tools the controller program has matured from assembly into use. Supported stepping configurations have been tuned and evaluated in full-body simulations, the first pathway-deletion and drive-stimulus experiments have been run, the gait-validation sweep has measured how far the tuned networks remain from the diversity of human reference locomotion, and the contact-driven rhythm core has been shown to transfer between independent implementations with exact period agreement and trace correlations above 0.9999. In hardware, the companion balance-platform studies demonstrated that a BPA-actuated limb holds itself upright under pseudo-vestibular inertial feedback \citep{bolen_balance_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_srs_2024}. What remains unfinished is equally specific, and it organizes the future work below: the balance and weight-bearing layers that unsupported bilateral walking requires, wider transfer across reference cycles, the supraspinal sensorium, and embedded real-time execution.
+The fourth contribution is the set of neuromechanical modeling tools in Chapter~\ref{ch:sns}. The Li--Deng walker, Gait2392-based controller implementations, and BPA-force integrations provide defined combinations of neural and mechanical models. SNS-Simscape makes the neural pathways editable as block diagrams, and the experiment drivers vary support, neural drive, sensory pathways, and external perturbations. These tools address the difficulty of determining whether a change in movement arises from neural organization, actuator mechanics, or implementation differences. Matched rhythm-core tests establish agreement between selected NumPy and Simulink implementations; the loaded-arm demonstration shows corrective feedback; and the fixed-controller W2L ablation shows that the tested contact and force-feedback pathways sustain its supported stepping. The contribution is both the reusable experimental capability and these specific demonstrations of what it can distinguish.
+
+The coupled-model results must be interpreted under their stated support conditions. Supported stepping and forward progression establish functioning neural--mechanical loops, while removal of tonic drive tests a different dependence from removal of mechanical support. The balance-platform studies provide a separate hardware demonstration of a BPA-actuated limb under pseudo-vestibular inertial feedback \citep{bolen_balance_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_2024, bolen_selfbalancing_srs_2024}. Together, the simulation tools and hardware precedents support further study of balance and weight bearing without treating those capabilities as already demonstrated by the stepping tests.
 
